1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:05,263
Reading Objects in a Museum A museum display showed an Egyptian sculpture

2
00:00:05,276 --> 00:00:09,961
beside a Roman coin Nearby stood a model of a medieval fortress

3
00:00:10,105 --> 00:00:14,908
complete with a tower a gate and a defensive trench The labels

4
00:00:14,921 --> 00:00:17,987
described the reign of a monarch the privileges of a duke and

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,592
a baron and the daily work of an ordinary tradesman A historian

6
00:00:22,605 --> 00:00:26,829
explained that a grand monument might glorify a conqueror while revealing little

7
00:00:26,842 --> 00:00:32,921
about those who built it The next section examined colonial expansion It

8
00:00:32,934 --> 00:00:36,303
distinguished the account of a colonist from the memory preserved by a

9
00:00:36,316 --> 00:00:42,026
descendant of a displaced community A military victory the guide observed could

10
00:00:42,039 --> 00:00:46,526
be followed by exile loss and a profound change in social life

11
00:00:47,434 --> 00:00:52,829
An exhibit should not erase that complexity Seen in context an object

12
00:00:52,842 --> 00:00:57,105
can illuminate more than its maker's skill It can also reveal who

13
00:00:57,118 --> 00:01:02,855
possessed authority and whose labour remained largely invisible The museum invited visitors

14
00:01:02,868 --> 00:01:07,750
to compare sources question a triumphant narration and consider the people missing

15
00:01:07,763 --> 00:01:13,079
from it History becomes more informative when admiration is accompanied by careful

16
00:01:13,092 --> 00:01:13,790
inquiry
